THE DATA THIS SOFTWARE DOWNLOADS IS NOT COVERED BY THAT LICENCE
================================================================================

The Apache 2.0 grant above covers this software. It does not cover, and cannot
grant any rights over, the statistical data the software retrieves. Each series
carries its own terms from the body that published it.

Those terms travel with the data itself, in every row of the series layer and of
any exported snapshot:

    license_id          which licence applies
    license_name        its name
    license_url         where to read it
    license_detail      the source's own status string
    attribution         the exact citation that source asks for
    license_notes       caveats
    origin_agencies     who actually produced the numbers
    origin_us_federal   whether a US federal body did, which decides copyright

In outline, as verified in September 2026:

  Eurostat   Free re-use for commercial and non-commercial purposes provided the
             source is acknowledged (Commission Decision 2011/833/EU).
             https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/main/help/copyright-notice

  OECD       Extraction, adaptation and distribution for any purpose including
             commercial, with the citation given in the metadata. Third-party
             data within OECD datasets is excepted and may carry other terms.
             https://www.oecd.org/en/about/terms-conditions.html

  FRED       A mixture, and the one that needs care. Series FRED marks
             "Public Domain: Citation Requested" whose originating body is a US
             federal one carry no copyright of their own (17 U.S.C. 105).
             Everything else is copyrighted by the agency that produced it and
             may not be redistributable. FRED itself republishes OECD, World
             Bank, IMF, BIS and Eurostat series, which keep their own terms.
             https://fred.stlouisfed.org/legal/

`terrastat export --public-only` selects the subset that may be redistributed and
writes an ATTRIBUTIONS.md beside it with the citations those sources require.
Without that flag, the export prints a warning naming every licence included —
but the filter, not the warning, is what makes a snapshot safe to publish.

Determining whether a particular use is permitted remains the responsibility of
whoever publishes the data. Nothing here is legal advice.
